<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Juan José Arévalo</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">1904–1990</span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Juan José Arévalo was a Guatemalan educator and philosopher who became the country's first democratically elected president after the revolution of 1944, serving from 1945 to 1951. His government adopted a new constitution and introduced social security, a labour code and autonomy for the University of San Carlos, under a programme he called 'spiritual socialism'. He survived many coup attempts and was succeeded by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jacobo-arbenz">Jacobo Árbenz</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/juan-jose-arevalo.jpg" alt="Juan José Arévalo" width="320" height="427" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Estudio Fleischmann · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Retrato_de_estudio_del_Dr._Juan_Jos%C3%A9_Ar%C3%A9valo_Bermejo_(cropped).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
